  "num_turns": null,
  "final_message": "I fixed the bug in `session_ttl.py`, and `python3 -m unittest -v test_public.py` now passes.\n\n**Bug:** `session_ttl_expired` removed the timezone from `now` (`.replace(tzinfo=None)`) but not from `created`. Subtracting the two then raised `TypeError: can't subtract offset-naive and offset-aware datetimes`.\n\n**Fix:** I deleted that `.replace(tzinfo=None)` so both timestamps are parsed the same way. When both carry timezones, the subtraction now correctly accounts for different offsets.\n\nExtra checks I ran:\n- 30 seconds after creation with a 60-second limit returns `False`.\n- Two timestamps with no timezone, 60 seconds apart, return `True`.\n- `+00:00` compared with `+01:00` is handled correctly (returns `False`, since only 30 seconds really passed).\n\nA timestamp with a timezone compared against one without still raises a `TypeError`. The README doesn't say how that case should behave, so I left it alone.\n\nThe formatter flags the file because of an existing long line (the `ttl_seconds` check). I didn't reformat it, to keep the change to the one fix.\n\nDONE: Stopped `session_ttl_expired` removing the timezone from only one timestamp; timezone-aware ISO inputs now compare correctly and the public test passes.",
