      "final_message": "I rewrote `merge_intervals` in `solution.py` so it follows the README, and both tests in `python3 -m unittest -v test_public.py` pass.\n\nWhat the function now does:\n- **Checks the input first:** a non-list raises `TypeError`. Any interval that isn't a two-element list or tuple of plain ints with `start <= end` raises `ValueError`. Bools are rejected too.\n- **Merges:** it sorts by start and joins an interval into the previous one when it begins at or before that one's end. Touching intervals merge, so `[[1, 2], [2, 3]]` becomes `[[1, 3]]`.\n- **Leaves the input alone:** it builds and returns a new list of `[start, end]` lists and never changes the caller's list or its items.\n\nBeyond the two public tests, I also ran these by hand: empty input, touching intervals, bool and float values, a wrong-length interval, a tuple passed instead of a list, and a check that the input is unchanged afterwards.\n\nDONE: Repaired `merge_intervals` to validate input, sort and merge overlapping or touching intervals into a new list without changing the input; public tests pass.",
